WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
A nonconformity is a failure to meet a requirement applicable to the AIMS.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:19.720
It may come from an internal audit, external audit, monitoring, incident review, complaint, management review, supplier failure, missed assessment, uncontrolled change, incomplete evidence, or repeated policy exception.

3
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.620
The first requirement is not blame.

4
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.240
It is clear evidence, ownership, and containment.

5
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.460
A well-run process separates the fact of nonconformity from assumptions about cause.

6
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.780
Correction and corrective action are different.

7
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:41.280
Correction fixes the detected condition, such as completing a missing supplier review or restoring a monitoring report.

8
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:45.620
Corrective action addresses the cause so the problem is less likely to recur.

9
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:52.600
Weak root-cause statements such as human error, lack of attention, or the model behaved unexpectedly rarely improve the system.

10
00:00:52.700 --> 00:01:05.100
The analysis should examine process design, authority, competence, supplier behavior, data, tooling, monitoring, incentives, and governance escalation.

11
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.320
This keeps the organization from solving only the visible symptom.

12
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:28.760
An effective corrective-action process identifies and contains the issue, records evidence, corrects the immediate condition, analyzes cause, determines broader exposure, selects action, assigns ownership, implements the action, verifies effectiveness, and updates risks, controls, and documented information.

13
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.220
Verification matters.

14
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.400
If the same exception repeats next quarter, the organization may have closed the record without fixing the system.

15
00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.120
Continual improvement is broader than audit findings.

16
00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:54.220
Improvement may come from new technology, incidents, monitoring trends, regulatory change, stakeholder feedback, supplier change, organizational restructuring, better evaluation methods, or lessons from near misses.

17
00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:03.360
An AIMS can share processes with information security, privacy, quality, risk, business continuity, supplier management, or software lifecycle governance.

18
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:16.740
Shared document control, competence, internal audit, management review, incident management, and corrective action can be efficient, but AI-specific risks and impacts must remain visible.

19
00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:20.600
Integration should reduce duplication without erasing the subject matter.

20
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.720
Certification readiness requires an operating management system, not a document rush before an audit.

21
00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:43.860
The organization should obtain an authorized copy of the standard, confirm scope, complete a gap assessment, operate required processes, preserve evidence, complete internal audits, conduct management review, address nonconformities, and select a competent certification body if certification is desired.

22
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.200
Certification is voluntary unless another obligation applies.

23
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:54.940
ISO does not certify organizations, and certification does not guarantee safe or lawful AI behavior.

24
00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.360
The stronger outcome is a better organizational process for AI decisions.

25
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:08.180
Certification readiness is strongest when the organization can show ordinary operating evidence over time, including issues found, actions taken, and improvements verified.

26
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.880
That evidence also helps leadership see whether the AIMS is becoming more suitable and effective.
